1. Pagination (your answer 👍)

Load data in chunks (say 20 items per page).

Reduces memory and improves performance.

Example: Page numbers or “Next/Previous” buttons.

🔹 2. Infinite Scrolling (Lazy Loading)

Instead of page numbers, load more data as the user scrolls down.

Like Instagram or Twitter feeds.

Reduces initial load, smooth user experience.
Example:
import { useState, useEffect } from "react";

function InfiniteScrollList() {
  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);
  const [page, setPage] = useState(1);

  // Fetch items from server
  const fetchData = async () => {
    const res = await fetch(`https://example.com/api/items?page=${page}`);
    const data = await res.json();
    setItems((prev) => [...prev, ...data]); // append new items
  };

  useEffect(() => {
    fetchData();
  }, [page]);

  // Detect scroll near bottom
  const handleScroll = () => {
    if (window.innerHeight + window.scrollY >= document.body.offsetHeight - 100) {
      setPage((prev) => prev + 1); // load next page
    }
  };

  useEffect(() => {
    window.addEventListener("scroll", handleScroll);
    return () => window.removeEventListener("scroll", handleScroll);
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      {items.map((item, i) => (
        <div key={i} style={{ padding: 20, border: "1px solid #ccc" }}>
          {item.name}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}


🔹 3. Virtualization / Windowing

Render only the items visible on screen, not all 1000 at once.

Libraries: react-window, react-virtualized.

Example: Even if you have 1000 rows, only ~20 are in the DOM at a time → huge performance boost.

import { FixedSizeList as List } from "react-window";

function UserList({ users }) {
  return (
    <List
      height={500}      // viewport height
      itemCount={users.length} 
      itemSize={50}     // row height
      width={300}
    >
      {({ index, style }) => (
        <div style={style}>
          {users[index].name}
        </div>
      )}
    </List>
  );
}

🔹 4. Data Caching

Use libraries like React Query, SWR, Apollo to cache data from the server.

Prevents unnecessary re-fetching when navigating back & forth.

Example: If user visits “page 2” again, data is already cached.

